    {
      "text": "It passed the basic tests, but it was not correct. It broke on the concurrency. It broke on the process failure. It broke on the network failure.",
      "timestamp_sec": 292,
      "why": "specifies exactly how agent-generated distributed code fails"
    },
    {
      "text": "The agent helped us build the system, but the agent did not help us design the system.",
      "timestamp_sec": 346,
      "why": "names the limitation that motivates the whole simulation approach"
    },
    {
      "text": "Do not build the production system. Build a simulated implementation. The simulated implementation is not the product. It is executable design.",
      "timestamp_sec": 410,
      "why": "the central methodological move, in one line"
    },
    {
      "text": "Unfortunately, minimalism and simplicity are not the starting point. They are the finish line. We spent 3 years making the protocol smaller and simpler.",
      "timestamp_sec": 478,
      "why": "a cost number attached to the precondition others will want to skip"
    },
    {
      "text": "Every time we ran into a problem, we asked, what can we take away? What abstraction can we erase? What property can we remove? What relationship can we break?",
      "timestamp_sec": 478,
      "why": "an actionable heuristic for shrinking a protocol"
    },
    {
      "text": "our implementation cannot be correct only when the target behaves conveniently. The implementation has to be correct when the target behaves legally.",
      "timestamp_sec": 604,
      "why": "the correctness standard, stated as a tradeoff against happy-path testing"
    },
    {
      "text": "Building always correct applications on top of a concurrency model that allows occasional stale reads is not simple. Not for humans.",
      "timestamp_sec": 662,
      "why": "concedes the difficulty is intrinsic, not an agent deficiency"
    },
    {
      "text": "Agents thrive on feedback. Immediate unambiguous feedback. Not just feedback that shows this went wrong. Feedback that shows why and how this went wrong.",
      "timestamp_sec": 717,
      "why": "the design principle behind the simulation tooling"
    },
    {
      "text": "unlike the real target, the simulation is deterministic, it's repeatable, and it's inspectable. So, when the agent writes the wrong algorithm, we can reproduce the exact execution that broke it.",
